1
00:00:16,623 --> 00:00:43,342
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in one, on one recent occasion, you said, I might be paraphrasing slightly, but you said, basically, just purify each and every thought in its arising, don't do anything unwholesome, the slightest unwholesome thing, and that's the practice.

2
00:00:44,522 --> 00:00:44,943
[SPEAKER_00]: And then,

3
00:00:46,173 --> 00:00:50,375
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's kind of one brief description of what the practice is.

4
00:00:52,817 --> 00:01:15,430
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, another kind of description of what the practice is, that has also come up often on other occasions, is that the practice basically constitutes... consists of developing complete understanding of the nature of things, or, like, completely undoing every misunderstanding.

5
00:01:16,760 --> 00:01:26,466
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the question is not, like, is in regards to how to understand what the practice is.

6
00:01:26,546 --> 00:01:36,992
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, I think the question that could be asked about this is, are these actually two, like, describing two different things?

7
00:01:38,033 --> 00:01:43,976
[SPEAKER_00]: Or is it actually a description of the exact same thing from two slightly different perspectives?

8
00:01:46,508 --> 00:02:03,476
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, so like, for example, that kind of previous type of view that I used to have was much, well, seemingly much closer to the first of those things that I mentioned.

9
00:02:03,496 --> 00:02:05,177
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, just don't do anything unwholesome.

10
00:02:06,297 --> 00:02:11,840
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, but it didn't occur to me, and I thought, okay, the one who has just purified everything,

11
00:02:12,613 --> 00:02:13,674
[SPEAKER_00]: We'll understand.

12
00:02:14,194 --> 00:02:17,876
[SPEAKER_00]: We'll have full knowledge and understanding by virtue of his purification.

13
00:02:19,597 --> 00:02:23,219
[SPEAKER_00]: But it didn't occur to me that... You don't know what purification is?

14
00:02:23,299 --> 00:02:37,788
[SPEAKER_00]: That I don't know what purification is, or that I could actually directly develop or try to understand the nature of things, and that this could actually also constitute purification.

15
00:02:38,668 --> 00:02:42,950
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, in what way are those two things related?

16
00:02:43,290 --> 00:02:43,710
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

17
00:02:44,531 --> 00:02:54,495
[SPEAKER_02]: So, like, the first thing you said, yeah, it's kind of true, but you realize then that you don't know what purification is.

18
00:02:55,275 --> 00:02:58,037
[SPEAKER_02]: And knowing what purification is, it's already a form of you.

19
00:03:00,628 --> 00:03:14,964
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you will not purify your views for as long as your behavior is not purified.

20
00:03:15,604 --> 00:03:16,485
[SPEAKER_02]: It simply won't happen.

21
00:03:17,026 --> 00:03:20,570
[SPEAKER_02]: But you won't even start purifying your behavior for as long as your views are not upgraded.

22
00:03:23,034 --> 00:03:26,175
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why it's a bit of both, in a way.

23
00:03:26,595 --> 00:03:31,056
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, a person first needs to know what to emphasize, i.e.

24
00:03:31,096 --> 00:03:34,997
[SPEAKER_02]: behavior, and learning what's unwholesome, and not doing anything.

25
00:03:36,337 --> 00:03:47,599
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, to emphasizing that, all the other views and misconceptions that they might have, by not emphasizing them, and by emphasizing behavior, they will be purified.

26
00:03:47,779 --> 00:03:50,180
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's like the faith aspect, to some extent.

27
00:03:55,085 --> 00:04:04,656
[SPEAKER_02]: You just have to figure out the fundamental, the root ones, that are taking you away from emphasizing the right thing, such as purification, and what purification is.

28
00:04:05,917 --> 00:04:10,701
[SPEAKER_02]: See, the example you gave, like, you used to thought, ah, I just need to purify everything.

29
00:04:11,162 --> 00:04:20,570
[SPEAKER_02]: I bet that meant I need to stop having these thoughts, and not have them arise, and always counter them, and always be there, ready, on point, whenever this happens, I'll deal with this.

30
00:04:21,071 --> 00:04:22,112
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's not purification.

31
00:04:22,132 --> 00:04:22,652
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, exactly.

32
00:04:22,672 --> 00:04:24,174
[SPEAKER_00]: Those very strong views are countered.

33
00:04:24,194 --> 00:04:31,820
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why it's kind of a bit of both, because, yes, if somebody is truly authentic, they will understand the first thing you said,

34
00:04:32,661 --> 00:04:35,663
[SPEAKER_02]: And they won't misconceive it, but most people are not.

35
00:04:36,584 --> 00:04:39,846
[SPEAKER_02]: And the first thing you said, it's much more easier to misconceive.

36
00:04:40,046 --> 00:04:52,395
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, yeah, I'm wholesome, sure, so I'll just be a good person, and I don't need to worry about any of the doubts, any of the views, any of the other things that are kind of... that my existence revolves around.

37
00:04:53,276 --> 00:04:55,958
[SPEAKER_02]: No, you do need to, because you don't know what purification is.

38
00:04:56,365 --> 00:04:57,466
[SPEAKER_02]: So, yes, you got the idea.

39
00:04:57,846 --> 00:05:05,690
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, I'll just purify wholesome and I'll just purify everything myself from unwholesome and won't do anything that's rooted in greed or aversion, delusion.

40
00:05:06,230 --> 00:05:08,051
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like, yeah, but do you know the extent of that?

41
00:05:08,512 --> 00:05:09,933
[SPEAKER_02]: And do you know what purification is?

42
00:05:10,093 --> 00:05:12,794
[SPEAKER_02]: Is it not having thoughts of greed, aversion, delusion?

43
00:05:12,894 --> 00:05:15,376
[SPEAKER_02]: Or is it not acting out of thoughts that are pressuring you?

44
00:05:16,416 --> 00:05:21,599
[SPEAKER_02]: That's already something that person needs to repeatedly learn and relearn to recognize that

45
00:05:22,106 --> 00:05:29,873
[SPEAKER_02]: Where that threshold of their responsibility is, as we keep saying, you know, you have to do things rightly and so on, and then these things can become apparent.

46
00:05:30,474 --> 00:05:43,305
[SPEAKER_02]: So, and all that would be known on account of upgrading your views, on account of reflecting rightly, pondering, discerning, contemplating, and then that the first part that you need to do becomes clearer.

47
00:05:44,246 --> 00:05:48,470
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, of course, if all you do is just chase around things that are unclear and

48
00:05:49,147 --> 00:05:57,774
[SPEAKER_02]: Try to kind of assuage your own doubt all the time and ignore the emphasis on the purification of behavior, the speech and thoughts.

49
00:05:57,914 --> 00:06:00,236
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, then, you won't purify your thoughts.

50
00:06:01,097 --> 00:06:07,322
[SPEAKER_02]: You might have them theoretically all lined up perfectly, but your behavior is still carrying the fuel for the wrong views.

51
00:06:08,475 --> 00:06:10,215
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's that, the relay chariot.

52
00:06:11,035 --> 00:06:21,097
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the views are purified on account of behavior, on account of virtue that leads to the development of samādhi, that then leads to the purification of view, the eye dropping all the wrong views.

53
00:06:24,138 --> 00:06:35,460
[SPEAKER_02]: So, today, emphasizing the virtue and samādhi that comes out of it rightly, like being able to endure the pressure of sensuality or will not act out of it,

54
00:06:35,900 --> 00:06:37,001
[SPEAKER_02]: See its full extent.

55
00:06:37,161 --> 00:06:38,602
[SPEAKER_02]: Learn how not to be moved by it.

56
00:06:39,884 --> 00:06:43,227
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, back in the day, somebody could have done that on account of faith alone.

57
00:06:43,487 --> 00:06:47,771
[SPEAKER_02]: The Buddha would tell them and they wouldn't need... they would not pay attention to any doubts that they have.

58
00:06:47,811 --> 00:06:48,731
[SPEAKER_02]: They would just stick to it.

59
00:06:49,172 --> 00:06:50,533
[SPEAKER_02]: Today, it's highly unlikely.

60
00:06:51,234 --> 00:06:54,697
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you would need to really clarify why this is so.

61
00:06:55,397 --> 00:06:57,059
[SPEAKER_02]: But then, you have to start doing it.

62
00:06:58,460 --> 00:06:59,641
[SPEAKER_02]: You have to start enduring it.

63
00:07:01,703 --> 00:07:01,883
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

64
00:07:03,522 --> 00:07:26,187
[SPEAKER_00]: I was also thinking that there's like a kind of corollary to that aspect of this is also the fact that, for example, it might be said that you just develop understanding of the nature of the sense bases, for example, you know, nature of eye, ear.

65
00:07:28,487 --> 00:07:32,228
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, if you genuinely want to try and understand the nature of

66
00:07:33,417 --> 00:07:35,618
[SPEAKER_00]: What is your eye, for example?

67
00:07:35,738 --> 00:07:37,199
[SPEAKER_00]: What's the nature of your senses?

68
00:07:37,639 --> 00:07:38,479
[SPEAKER_00]: Are they impermanent?

69
00:07:38,820 --> 00:07:39,680
[SPEAKER_00]: What does that even mean?

70
00:07:40,981 --> 00:07:53,206
[SPEAKER_00]: To develop practical understanding of that, it becomes quite clear, quite quickly, that it's completely incompatible.

71
00:07:54,067 --> 00:08:00,450
[SPEAKER_00]: Throughout the day, having this as a kind of a contemplation, it's completely incompatible with acting out of your senses.

72
00:08:05,725 --> 00:08:22,255
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're honestly, genuinely trying to understand that thing, it naturally kind of pertains to something that is, yeah, kind of incompatible with the attitude of greed and aversion and delusion.

73
00:08:23,015 --> 00:08:26,637
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in that sense as well, it could be said that

74
00:08:29,122 --> 00:08:37,468
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, correctly developing understanding of things has to, kind of, naturally comes to include a degree of purification.

75
00:08:37,488 --> 00:08:41,511
[SPEAKER_00]: There are things that have to be thought of as separate, or almost never are.

76
00:08:41,711 --> 00:08:46,894
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's also, like, where different characters, different people, different faculties would come into play.

77
00:08:47,615 --> 00:08:54,800
[SPEAKER_02]: The, eh... Somebody might naturally have more, more of, like, kind of affinity towards

78
00:08:57,520 --> 00:09:00,121
[SPEAKER_02]: So, clarity, contemplation, reflection, approach.

79
00:09:01,101 --> 00:09:10,984
[SPEAKER_02]: But, yeah, even they, if they're honest, they would recognize, well, there is nothing to me, there's nothing for me to apply this, this approach and reflection, unless my behavior is being purified.

80
00:09:11,864 --> 00:09:14,685
[SPEAKER_02]: Unless virtue and sense restraint is my mode of being.

81
00:09:16,105 --> 00:09:21,827
[SPEAKER_02]: Somebody might be, like, less reflective, less doubtful, less inclined to think about these things.

82
00:09:22,547 --> 00:09:23,248
[SPEAKER_02]: But then they would.

83
00:09:25,052 --> 00:09:32,714
[SPEAKER_02]: See, then, in broad strokes, on account of doing the work of purification and samādhi, or in composure.

84
00:09:32,894 --> 00:09:42,157
[SPEAKER_02]: So, whichever one you put, sort of, forward as your way of engaging with, towards getting understanding, the other one will have to follow.

85
00:09:42,597 --> 00:09:43,798
[SPEAKER_02]: But, fundamentally, yeah.

86
00:09:45,245 --> 00:09:56,533
[SPEAKER_02]: The order of development is, like, you need to have had virtue established for quite a while, sense restraint, to be able to contain that pressure, see it, not act out of it.

87
00:09:57,234 --> 00:10:06,221
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, whichever aspect of practice your inclinations might put first, well, it can come to some sort of fruition, because, as I said, you have contained it.

88
00:10:07,528 --> 00:10:10,749
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's the, again, the order of the gradual training, yeah.

89
00:10:11,230 --> 00:10:16,592
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that doesn't mean that, like, okay, so until I'm developed in virtue, I should not think or read any sutta.

90
00:10:16,612 --> 00:10:19,053
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, that's silly, because that's already a view.

91
00:10:20,137 --> 00:10:22,859
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't start with neutral views, you start with lots of views.

92
00:10:22,919 --> 00:10:30,463
[SPEAKER_02]: So, yeah, read the suttas and stuff, just don't forget what comes first and what holds it underneath, what type of development, and there is no compromise there.

93
00:10:31,283 --> 00:10:46,011
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, you might doubt this sutta, that sutta, who said, you know, what in regard to, you know, I don't know, five aggregates or something, but you don't doubt that priests come first and sense restraint and guarding of the door, sense doors and watchfulness and so on.

94
00:10:46,292 --> 00:10:48,533
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's... and through doing that,

95
00:10:49,377 --> 00:10:52,618
[SPEAKER_02]: Your views are being clarified.

96
00:10:53,098 --> 00:10:59,659
[SPEAKER_02]: And through clarifying your views, keeping the precepts, enduring the pressure, not being moved on account of it, will become easier.

97
00:11:00,119 --> 00:11:01,680
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it just fits.

98
00:11:01,700 --> 00:11:02,360
[SPEAKER_02]: It makes more sense.

99
00:11:02,620 --> 00:11:04,580
[SPEAKER_02]: You're not resisting that idea anymore.

100
00:11:04,760 --> 00:11:05,861
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not bothering you as much.

101
00:11:09,101 --> 00:11:15,803
[SPEAKER_00]: I was thinking, like... One of the reasons I was thinking about this was just, like, if you read Nenevira, for example,

102
00:11:16,998 --> 00:11:23,080
[SPEAKER_00]: He almost never talks or mentions explicitly, you know, sense restraint and virtue and so on.

103
00:11:23,120 --> 00:11:26,901
[SPEAKER_00]: He's all about really understanding.

104
00:11:27,921 --> 00:11:34,843
[SPEAKER_00]: And, you know, one could kind of then mistakenly get the impression from that that, oh, well, that's kind of all he was doing.

105
00:11:35,804 --> 00:11:46,387
[SPEAKER_00]: But, at the same, if you really read it properly, it's very obvious that none of the... you know, he's able to describe

106
00:11:47,471 --> 00:11:55,313
[SPEAKER_00]: The very subtlest forms of bad faith on the level of the tiniest view or thoughts, that's obviously impossible.

107
00:11:55,593 --> 00:11:56,033
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

108
00:11:56,053 --> 00:12:02,994
[SPEAKER_00]: That could be seen or understood without having... All one needs to do is just have a look and see how he lived.

109
00:12:03,034 --> 00:12:03,514
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

110
00:12:05,055 --> 00:12:07,815
[SPEAKER_02]: He lived in, you know, quite austere, remote area.

111
00:12:07,875 --> 00:12:12,776
[SPEAKER_02]: Even today, these days, it's quite arid in that area of Sri Lanka.

112
00:12:12,796 --> 00:12:14,537
[SPEAKER_02]: But back then, it was even worse.

113
00:12:14,697 --> 00:12:15,497
[SPEAKER_02]: And he lived there.

114
00:12:17,142 --> 00:12:22,353
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that already takes commitment and composure and so on.

115
00:12:23,034 --> 00:12:25,039
[SPEAKER_02]: But, yes, as an instruction,

116
00:12:27,592 --> 00:12:48,356
[SPEAKER_02]: In Nyanavira's teaching, you realize, yeah, it can... people can easily overlook, and they do overlook, especially people with intellectual tendencies, they tend to, you know, really, like... like folks, but overlook the entire aspect of the lifestyle underneath everything that Nyanavira thought and... and views he had and teachings he conveyed.

117
00:12:48,376 --> 00:12:53,717
[SPEAKER_02]: So, people will be doing PhD in Nyanavira and stuff, which is like a... quite a contradiction in terms.

118
00:12:54,941 --> 00:13:06,448
[SPEAKER_02]: Um, but... and he mentions in passing, I remember, in some ways, like, that, of course, the virtue and sense restraint, that's... they don't... he doesn't even talk about it in the book, because it's just taken for granted, that you have to do it.

119
00:13:06,729 --> 00:13:06,829
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

120
00:13:07,129 --> 00:13:24,380
[SPEAKER_02]: But you realize, when when people, new people, are approaching it, it's it's not... it's not necessarily wasteful to to mention it and re-establish it as the necessary basis, because if you do establish it, then tenth of your effort into understanding Indraveera and clarifying all those ideas he had

121
00:13:24,910 --> 00:13:27,553
[SPEAKER_02]: will be more than enough for what you're trying to achieve.

122
00:13:28,514 --> 00:13:44,130
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you're not developed in virtue, not efficiently, not efficiently restrained, then you could be, you know, revolving day and night and still not really land on anything that matters, so to speak, because the basis is not there.

123
00:13:44,730 --> 00:13:49,931
[SPEAKER_02]: Fuel is still being provided inadvertently to all the views and misconceptions.

124
00:13:49,971 --> 00:13:53,052
[SPEAKER_02]: So, in other words, all your effort is applied theoretically.

125
00:13:54,032 --> 00:13:54,632
[SPEAKER_02]: It's over there.

126
00:13:55,212 --> 00:14:07,955
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, it can also then lead to further worsening of your wrong views whereby now you think that you understood both Nyanavir and the Buddha, while still even your basic virtue and sense restraint and not acting out of unwholesome mental states is not even purified.

127
00:14:09,395 --> 00:14:13,916
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I remember finding that actually quite

128
00:14:14,716 --> 00:14:33,722
[SPEAKER_00]: It's sort of refreshing, in a way, the fact that he kind of finds it so obvious, or takes it so much for granted, that the types of lifestyle and the virtue that he needs, that he doesn't even kind of feel the need to mention it, but it's totally implied, implicit in everything that is said.

129
00:14:34,422 --> 00:14:42,085
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, that it's like, kind of refreshing from the point of view of, you know, there's so much

130
00:14:43,520 --> 00:15:04,910
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know, views and discussion over what constitutes even even virtue and, you know, you can think it's a very virtuous and it's all... by just merely kind of living a lifestyle that is not even close to even what he was living, but for him it was just, well, like, of course I have to do that, but that's not even even close to enough, because then you still need to develop understanding.

131
00:15:06,271 --> 00:15:08,032
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, it's like the discerning that

132
00:15:10,497 --> 00:15:35,015
[SPEAKER_02]: Find middle line between just adhering to the prescribed sets of behavior on account of belief that this is leading me closer to the light of nibbāna, or just falling into that completely, just explanatory, psychologizing, everything must be answered and fit completely out of your mental craving, but you don't see that.

133
00:15:35,416 --> 00:15:39,719
[SPEAKER_02]: So, deciding the middle line would be like, yeah, so, if you are

134
00:15:40,802 --> 00:15:52,469
[SPEAKER_02]: Protecting certain types of behavior, guarding your speech, guarding your sense doors, for the sake of diminishing the views that you don't necessarily need to hunt down one by one.

135
00:15:53,510 --> 00:15:57,913
[SPEAKER_02]: You just need to stick to this behavior and then keep an eye on the general picture.

136
00:15:58,813 --> 00:16:00,755
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, is my mind now, since I've been

137
00:16:01,773 --> 00:16:08,897
[SPEAKER_02]: Investing on every precepts, enduring the pulls and the pressures about all some things, not acting out of it, seeing the danger in them.

138
00:16:09,437 --> 00:16:10,498
[SPEAKER_02]: Is my mind calmer?

139
00:16:11,338 --> 00:16:12,279
[SPEAKER_02]: Are things clearer?

140
00:16:13,379 --> 00:16:16,061
[SPEAKER_02]: When suffering arises, how long it persists?

141
00:16:16,601 --> 00:16:21,064
[SPEAKER_02]: How strong is the tendency for me to get rid of the arisen suffering when it arises?

142
00:16:21,244 --> 00:16:22,164
[SPEAKER_02]: Is that diminishing?

143
00:16:23,645 --> 00:16:25,906
[SPEAKER_02]: Am I more indifferent towards it?

144
00:16:25,946 --> 00:16:26,747
[SPEAKER_02]: Am I still afraid?

145
00:16:26,847 --> 00:16:27,867
[SPEAKER_02]: Am I still bothered by it?

146
00:16:27,908 --> 00:16:28,368
[SPEAKER_02]: Do I still

147
00:16:29,067 --> 00:16:30,688
[SPEAKER_02]: Really crave for it to end.

148
00:16:31,189 --> 00:16:34,552
[SPEAKER_02]: All of those little things are already showing you the development.

149
00:16:35,312 --> 00:16:53,027
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why, if somebody is not too concerned about answering, you know, every permutation that their mind might come up with, but instead protects behavior, develops this type of samādhi, of learning how to be movable in regard to things that want to move you,

150
00:16:54,082 --> 00:16:56,984
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, yeah, the craving is factually diminishing.

151
00:16:57,124 --> 00:17:00,827
[SPEAKER_02]: You're not feeling it, you're not providing any more, any more firewood for it.

152
00:17:01,567 --> 00:17:04,690
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, those, those phenomena will become obvious.

153
00:17:04,710 --> 00:17:07,231
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, look, my mind is not moved anymore.

154
00:17:07,271 --> 00:17:09,113
[SPEAKER_02]: And now you see, and now you see exactly why.

155
00:17:09,253 --> 00:17:10,494
[SPEAKER_02]: And there is no more doubt there.

156
00:17:11,735 --> 00:17:12,815
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, yeah, there is no more...

157
00:17:12,895 --> 00:17:13,596
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't crave.

158
00:17:13,956 --> 00:17:16,478
[SPEAKER_02]: When unpleasant feeling touches me, I don't move either way.

159
00:17:16,778 --> 00:17:23,403
[SPEAKER_02]: So why would I kick and scream internally to get rid of it and replace it with pleasure, when I already developed the value of immovability?

160
00:17:24,302 --> 00:17:32,355
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you could see how this type of development, through this correct samādhi, is not as unpleasant.

161
00:17:33,517 --> 00:17:37,263
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, either way, you have to do the pressure, but how much you will revolve around it...

162
00:17:38,468 --> 00:17:42,350
[SPEAKER_02]: How much will you, you know, be gripped by doubts and all the pain that comes with it?

163
00:17:43,291 --> 00:17:51,356
[SPEAKER_02]: That's... that's not... that's not going to be there, because you are emphasizing the behavior and the immovability and clarity in regard to that.

164
00:17:51,936 --> 00:17:59,361
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you're able to refrain your own self from... Oh, but let me just think about it and provide, you know, and clarify this further.

165
00:17:59,381 --> 00:17:59,661
[SPEAKER_02]: No, no, no.

166
00:17:59,681 --> 00:18:00,962
[SPEAKER_02]: Let me just think about what's right here.

167
00:18:01,282 --> 00:18:02,263
[SPEAKER_02]: What's right pressuring me?

168
00:18:02,783 --> 00:18:05,425
[SPEAKER_02]: What current state of mind is, that's the extent of my thought.

169
00:18:05,685 --> 00:18:08,728
[SPEAKER_02]: Not like, oh, I'll stop, think about it, and I'll just do these things blindly.

170
00:18:08,748 --> 00:18:10,529
[SPEAKER_02]: That would be, again, falling back onto that.

171
00:18:10,809 --> 00:18:13,772
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm just mechanically doing this behavior, expecting nibbāna to happen.

172
00:18:14,292 --> 00:18:14,652
[SPEAKER_02]: No, no.

173
00:18:14,912 --> 00:18:21,457
[SPEAKER_02]: You need to let the pressure in, to some extent, but then you also have a choice how far you will then go on account of it, mentally.

174
00:18:23,559 --> 00:18:29,784
[SPEAKER_02]: And that person can arrive at the same goal without having to suffer much, internally, on account of mental

175
00:18:33,554 --> 00:18:47,204
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, the other person, who also does the work, and also would then be giving in to the tendency to, you know, approach from different aspects, clarify, compare it, revisit it, see if he can doubt it, bring up the doubt, just to see if he's going to do all these things.

176
00:18:47,244 --> 00:18:48,565
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, that's going to cause more pain.

177
00:18:48,665 --> 00:18:50,866
[SPEAKER_02]: But, of course, he's factually exploring more.

178
00:18:51,727 --> 00:18:59,533
[SPEAKER_02]: So, although he will suffer more, he will have more familiarity with various different aspects of practice.

179
00:19:01,054 --> 00:19:03,516
[SPEAKER_02]: Fundamentally, he will arrive at the same goal.

180
00:19:04,176 --> 00:19:05,397
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's really the goal.

181
00:19:06,358 --> 00:19:09,760
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not like how many facets of the dhamma you are familiar with.

182
00:19:09,840 --> 00:19:13,963
[SPEAKER_02]: It's are you fully free from greed, delusion or not.

183
00:19:13,983 --> 00:19:23,490
[SPEAKER_02]: So that's why whether you arrive through faith or through your just sticking to the composure practice of allowing pressure to come in but not revolving around it too far,

184
00:19:24,727 --> 00:19:30,911
[SPEAKER_02]: Or, are you the type who will revolve around too far and then extend that endurance to that level and suffer on account of it?

185
00:19:32,272 --> 00:19:37,615
[SPEAKER_02]: Either way, these three types, whoever arrives first at being an arahant, is the best.

186
00:19:39,616 --> 00:19:39,976
[SPEAKER_02]: That's it.

187
00:19:41,017 --> 00:19:42,698
[SPEAKER_02]: Because that's really the measure of success.

188
00:19:46,140 --> 00:19:52,323
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a... there's actually a sutta in the... that I thought might be relevant to this.

189
00:19:52,443 --> 00:19:53,664
[SPEAKER_00]: I might... it's in this...

190
00:20:03,536 --> 00:20:12,958
[SPEAKER_00]: So, somebody is asking a question.

191
00:20:13,578 --> 00:20:17,359
[SPEAKER_00]: So, people say there are sages in the world, and how is that?

192
00:20:18,379 --> 00:20:23,600
[SPEAKER_00]: Do they call the one who qualifies through knowledge a sage, or else the one who qualifies through

193
00:20:24,598 --> 00:20:25,578
[SPEAKER_00]: Virtue or behavior?

194
00:20:27,099 --> 00:20:34,042
[SPEAKER_00]: And the answer is, neither through view, nor through learning or knowledge, do the skillful ones call a sage here.

195
00:20:35,843 --> 00:20:45,566
[SPEAKER_00]: Those who are unopposing, trouble-free and rid of hope, who live like this, these I call sages.

196
00:20:46,267 --> 00:20:47,427
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, the extinguished ones.

197
00:20:47,647 --> 00:20:47,947
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

198
00:20:48,047 --> 00:20:48,288
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

199
00:20:51,569 --> 00:20:53,930
[SPEAKER_00]: And so then it's...

200
00:20:55,129 --> 00:21:13,244
[SPEAKER_00]: Continues, he asks, so these recluses and Brahmins who declare purity through the scene and the herd, who declare purity through virtue and practices, declare purity in various other ways, I wonder if they, oh blessed one, living in accordance with that, if they have crossed birth and old age?

201
00:21:14,606 --> 00:21:23,033
[SPEAKER_00]: And the answer is, basically, that they have not crossed birth and old age, whoever declared purity in any of these ways.

202
00:21:25,813 --> 00:21:28,656
[SPEAKER_00]: And the answer, I'm kind of shortening it, is basically just...

203
00:21:29,577 --> 00:21:42,688
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if these who have declared purity in all of these various ways, and if you say that they have not crossed the flood, then who has crossed birth and old age?

204
00:21:43,970 --> 00:21:48,754
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the answer is, I do not say that all recluses and brahmins are in the grip of birth and old age.

205
00:21:49,638 --> 00:22:03,348
[SPEAKER_00]: Those who have abandoned the scene, the herd, thought, as well as virtue and practice, and have abandoned various other ways too, who have fully known craving and are taintless, they indeed are those who have crossed the flood.

206
00:22:08,291 --> 00:22:10,733
[SPEAKER_01]: So it's... yeah.

207
00:22:10,893 --> 00:22:16,096
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's the... that's the sutta, but that seemed to be kind of relating to what you were... Yeah.

208
00:22:16,136 --> 00:22:18,138
[SPEAKER_00]: It's more basically like saying a similar thing.

209
00:22:18,987 --> 00:22:38,839
[SPEAKER_00]: And a lot of these, actually, I find, they are kind of saying similar things, and in other ways, they always just come back to this very pertinent point that it all comes down to just abandoning craving in whatever, from whatever angle, particular angle, it doesn't really matter.

210
00:22:41,400 --> 00:22:45,503
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, obviously, that needs to be still understood, what that actually means.

211
00:22:48,317 --> 00:22:50,095
[SPEAKER_01]: It reminds me, in a way, of the cherry.

